---
/**
 * foodcraft / LocationBL — "VISIT US" (Kalinaw Coffee / BL).
 *
 * ROLE: the getting-here section. The design draws a two-part head — eyebrow +
 * the ADDRESS set as the Fraunces display headline on the left, an arrival note
 * on the right, both sitting on one bottom edge and wrapping before either can
 * overflow — and under it a 1.2fr / .8fr split: a plotted map face at 360px
 * beside a white OPENING HOURS card of day/time rows.
 *
 * THE DESIGN IS THE SPECIFICATION. Every metric below is lifted verbatim from
 * "Kalinaw Coffee Landing.dc.html", data-component="LOCATION":
 *   head    flex · align-items:flex-end · space-between · gap 30px · wrap
 *           margin-bottom 34px; h2 margin .26em 0 0; sub 15px/1.75 max-width 34ch
 *   split   minmax(0,1.2fr) minmax(0,.8fr) · gap 24px
 *   map     min-height 360px · radius 16px · ground #F7EFE1 · 1px #E9DFCF
 *           plot grid 44px at rgba(180,84,31,.08)
 *           roads  top 47% h:5px · left 44% w:4px · rgba(180,84,31,.22)
 *           pin    translate(-50%,-100%) off that crossing
 *           dot    14px #B4541F ringed 3px #FFFCF6
 *           bubble #241A12 / #FBF6EC · 9px/.2em caps · 5px 12px · 100px
 *           caption left 18px bottom 18px · 10px/.22em caps · #948373
 *   card    #FFFFFF · 1px #E9DFCF · radius 16px · padding 26px 28px
 *   row     space-between · gap 16px · padding 12px 0 · 1px #F2EADC
 *           day 13px #948373 · time 14px #241A12 right-aligned
 * NO LITERAL HEX APPEARS IN THIS FILE (R1). Every one of those values is written
 * as the PageBL token that already holds it: --paper-2 --line --line-3 --card
 * --ink --ink-soft --muted --accent --accent-rgb --band --bone --paper --r-lg
 * --r-pill --sans. Nothing is re-derived to survive an admin colour scheme —
 * that re-derivation is what shipped an earlier template looking like a
 * different design, and the wrapper's frontmatter already names --accent as the
 * one axis the theme engine is welcome to move.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS (every one declared in components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts
 * under the "Location" group, so the sidebar owns a real input for each — a
 * data-field on an undeclared path is a dead hook that focuses nothing):
 *   location.tag                     — the rust eyebrow (the wrapper's .kick)
 *   location.headline                — the h2, AND THE ADDRESS. Rendered with
 *                                      set:html so an owner's own <em> picks up
 *                                      PageBL's ITALIC RUST EMPHASIS. That rule
 *                                      is global in the wrapper on purpose:
 *                                      scoped styles cannot reach elements
 *                                      created by set:html (R3), so an italic
 *                                      rule written here would never apply.
 *   location.sub                     — the arrival note, right of the head
 *   location.rules.<original i>.label — the DAY cell of an hours row
 *   location.rules.<original i>.value — the TIME cell of an hours row
 *   location.hours                   — the card's closing hours note
 *   location.lat / location.lng      — feed the Leaflet boot. No data-field:
 *                                      they are coordinates, not copy, and the
 *                                      sidebar edits them as their own inputs.
 *
 * WHY location.rules AND NOT location.hours FOR THE DAY/TIME ROWS. The schema
 * declares `location.hours` as a single kind:'text' field ("Hours line") and
 * `location.rules` as the kind:'list' of {label,value} pairs. The design's card
 * is a REPEATING two-cell row, so it needs the list. Emitting
 * `location.hours.<i>.day` instead is not a style choice, it is the exact data
 * loss the schema's own comment at that field warns about and that
 * isSchemaEditablePath was written to stop: LocationBI shipped that path, the
 * deep writer converted the existing `location.hours` STRING into an array, and
 * every other hours row vanished on the next rebuild. So the rows are
 * location.rules.*, and location.hours keeps its own scalar slot below them.
 *
 * WHERE location.hours LANDS, and why not in the card's eyebrow. The design's
 * card opens with a literal "Opening hours" label and closes with a literal
 * caveat paragraph. Both are the DESIGNER filling in their own template (R2), so
 * neither ships as written and only one of the two slots has a hook behind it.
 * location.hours takes the CLOSING PARAGRAPH, not the label, for one concrete
 * reason: an owner who types only an hours line and adds no rows then gets a
 * card that still states their hours. Put the same string in the eyebrow and
 * that owner gets a tracked-caps label with nothing underneath it — a heading
 * over nothing, which is the thing R2 exists to prevent — and the card would
 * have to be gated on rows alone, dropping their hours off the page entirely.
 *
 * THE CARD THEREFORE HAS NO EYEBROW. That is a decision, not an oversight. No
 * schema path owns a title for it, and location.tag is already spent on the
 * section eyebrow above — a second data-field on that path would be two sidebar
 * inputs fighting over one value.
 *
 * DERIVED, printed but NOT editable here (each of these already owns an input in
 * the field that holds it, so a second hook on this section would be a duplicate
 * input fighting the first):
 *   layout.businessName   → the pin bubble (the design's "cafeName")
 *   layout.contact.city   → the map caption (the design's "cafeTown")
 *   layout.contact.address / .lat / .lng → fallbacks behind the location.* pair
 *
 * THE HEADLINE IS THE ADDRESS, AND THERE IS NO SECOND ADDRESS PARAGRAPH. The
 * design's head is eyebrow + h2 + sub — three elements, with the h2 set to
 * `{{ addressLine }}`. There is no fourth. LocationBK grew an address paragraph
 * under its h2 on the reasoning that derive-content-defaults.ts fills
 * location.headline with "Where to find us" on every site while the real street
 * line sits in location.address, and it had to be taken out again: the head is a
 * flex row whose two cells share a bottom edge, so a two-line paragraph in the
 * left cell drags the sub down a row and strands it on the right beside nothing.
 * That paragraph is not built here.
 *
 * The address is not lost by leaving it out. It feeds the geocode below — tier 2
 * of the map, which is how this very section finds its own pin — and FooterBL
 * prints it in the contact column, so location.address keeps a live sidebar
 * input either way. An owner who wants their street line as the heading pastes
 * it into the headline, which is precisely what the design does.
 *
 * DELIBERATELY NOT RENDERED:
 *   · location.phone. The design's LOCATION prints no number; the closing band
 *     and the footer own it. A tel: row here would be a control this design does
 *     not have (R2).
 *   · location.directions.text/href. Schema-declared and used by siblings, but
 *     this design's LOCATION has no directions button. Adding one would be
 *     building a different section than the one that was handed over.
 *   · NO .plate / .ph / role="img" (R9). The map cell holds no upload — it is a
 *     DRAWING of a map — so it gets neither the wrapper's image frame nor an
 *     image role. The only accessible name available for it would be a sentence
 *     I wrote ("Map showing …"), which is exactly the component-supplied copy R2
 *     and R9 forbid. The whole drawn face, pin and bubble included, is therefore
 *     aria-hidden once, on the face itself and nowhere else — a literal
 *     aria-hidden beside a conditional one is the duplicated-attribute bug two
 *     BK components shipped — and everything it pictures is announced as real
 *     text beside it: the address headline, the caption and the hours rows.
 *   · NO STAR ROW (R2). This section carries no rating field and no review. Five
 *     stars printed for a customer who gave no rating is a fabricated claim;
 *     stars belong to TESTIMONIALS and only ever from a numeric rating.
 *   · NO onPage() ANCHOR-REMAP HELPER (R7), and this too is a decision. The
 *     helper exists to rewrite in-page hrefs onto this page's ids, and this
 *     section emits NO href at all — the map is not a link, the card is not a
 *     link, and the design draws no button here. A copied helper would ship as
 *     dead code, which is why LocationBK, LocationBJ, WhyBL, GalleryBL and
 *     TestimonialsBL all leave it out too. If a link is ever added here, copy it
 *     in from a BL sibling with PAGE_ANCHORS = menu · about · gallery ·
 *     location · faq · main and ANCHOR_ALIAS mapping visit,contact → location ·
 *     services,rooms → menu · host,why → about · work → gallery · book → main,
 *     passing tel: / mailto: / http through untouched.
 *
 * SECTION GATE (R5). Body content only: the map (which needs real coordinates OR
 * a real address behind it), the hours rows, the hours note, or the arrival
 * paragraph. NOT the tag and NOT the headline — derive-content-defaults.ts emits
 * location.tag ('Visit') and location.headline ('Where to find us') for EVERY
 * site, so a gate that counted them would be always-true and would ship an
 * eyebrow and a heading over an empty two-column hole on every first build. The
 * split is gated again on its own, so a site with only a sub keeps its paragraph
 * and grows no empty grid under it, and either cell can leave on its own with
 * the survivor taking the full width rather than sitting beside a hole.
 *
 * THE MAP, three tiers, exactly the pattern LocationBK/BJ use:
 *   1. build-time lat/lng → window.__foodcraftBLInitMap(lat, lng, label)
 *   2. else any address   → client-side Nominatim lookup, then the same boot
 *   3. else               → the drawn plot-grid face, which IS the design
 * The drawn face lives INSIDE #map until Leaflet mounts; PageBL's boot runs
 * el.innerHTML = '' first, and that is what erases it. The literal id="map" and
 * the exact global name __foodcraftBLInitMap are a contract with PageBL — rename
 * either and the drawn face silently stays on screen underneath the tiles. The
 * name is family-unique because a preview harness can render two wrappers in one
 * document and a shared name cross-wires the maps.
 *
 * A CLEARED coordinate field leaves '', which passes != null and which Number('')
 * turns into 0 — booting Leaflet and pinning the café at 0,0 in the Gulf of
 * Guinea with its own name on the marker: a map asserting a location the owner
 * never gave. num() rejects blank and out-of-range values so a cleared field
 * falls through to the address lookup instead, and it uses ?? rather than || so
 * a genuine 0 survives.
 *
 * TWO FORCED DEVIATIONS, both small, both for the same reason — the design draws
 * this cell as a static picture and ours can turn into a live tile map:
 *   1. The pin bubble DROPS white-space: nowrap. The frame clips overflow, so a
 *      long business name would be sliced off at both ends; wrapping beats
 *      truncating on the element that names the café. The stack is centred on
 *      the 44% crossing, so it is capped at twice the smaller side — 88% — less
 *      12px of air each side, and width: max-content is required alongside that
 *      or absolute shrink-to-fit collapses it to (containing block − left).
 *   2. The caption gains an OPAQUE --paper-2 backing. In the design it sits on
 *      the drawn cream face; the moment Leaflet paints CARTO tiles underneath,
 *      unbacked 10px tracked caps are unreadable over street labels. The backing
 *      keeps the design's exact colour pair (--muted on --paper-2) rather than
 *      swapping in the wrapper's espresso .cap pill, which would have changed
 *      the design's palette to solve a legibility problem.
 *
 * THREE VALUES RESTATED OFF A WRAPPER PRIMITIVE. None is a new choice: each one
 * puts back a number the design states and the primitive rounds off.
 *   · The arrival note is a FLAT 15px. .body is clamp(14.5px,1.3vw,15px), which
 *     reaches the design's size only above a ~1150px viewport and prints 14.5px
 *     on every laptop below it.
 *   · The map caption is WEIGHT 400 and LEADING 1.3. The design's caption sets
 *     neither, so it inherits the body's regular and a normal line box — where
 *     .mono carries the wrapper's tracked-caps 500 and PageBL's body carries
 *     1.7, which would have made the added backing pill ~27px tall around 10px
 *     of text.
 *   · The hours rows are LEADING 1.3, the same leak: it is what holds the row at
 *     the ~42px the design draws rather than 45px or 48px.
 * ServicesBL restates the same 400 on .s-tag and TrustBL the same 1.3 on
 * .tally-lab, and ServicesBL/WhyBL both restate the flat 15px — this is the
 * family's existing habit with these two primitives, not a local invention.
 *
 * CONTRAST, stated rather than quietly fixed. The day cell and the caption are
 * --muted (#948373), the design's own value, ~3.6:1 on --card and --paper-2 —
 * below AA, and PageBL's frontmatter already records that --muted is the
 * design's palette and the owner's call. Note one honest difference from the
 * wrapper's stated rationale: it justifies --muted as "10-13px labels that the
 * heading beside them repeats", and the DAY cell is not repeated by anything —
 * it is the only place in this template where --muted carries unique
 * information. Darkening --muted to #7C6C5C in the wrapper clears it in one
 * line. It is not done HERE, because a section that recolours its own text away
 * from the design is the re-derivation this template is under orders not to
 * repeat; it is raised for the owner instead.
 *
 * SECTION PADDING is the wrapper's, not this file's. The design writes
 * `max-width:1240px;margin:0 auto;padding:96px 32px 0`: the 1240/gutter half is
 * the shared .wrap, and the 96px top is html[data-page="BL"] section's
 * clamp(56px,7.6vw,96px), which caps at exactly that.
 */
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content, layout } = Astro.props;
const lay = layout || {};

const loc = content.location || {};
const tag = typeof loc.tag === 'string' ? loc.tag.trim() : '';
// Nothing beyond trim(): the headline goes out through set:html so whatever
// markup the owner typed reaches the wrapper's italic-rust <em> rule.
const headline = typeof loc.headline === 'string' ? loc.headline.trim() : '';
const sub = typeof loc.sub === 'string' ? loc.sub.trim() : '';
// The card's closing note. The schema's single "Hours line" text field — see the
// frontmatter for why it lands here and not in the card's eyebrow.
const hoursNote = typeof loc.hours === 'string' ? loc.hours.trim() : '';

// READ, NOT PRINTED. The design's head is eyebrow + h2 + sub and its h2 IS the
// address; there is no second address paragraph anywhere in that section. This
// value feeds the geocode below (tier 2 of the map) and FooterBL prints it.
const address = String(loc.address ?? lay.contact?.address ?? '').trim();

// A row the owner added but never filled would spend a hairline and 24px of
// padding saying nothing. Drop the blanks (whitespace counts as blank) but carry
// the ORIGINAL index so deleting row 2 cannot misdirect an edit into row 3 (R6),
// and note that this filter is what makes the gate honest: a rules array of
// nothing but blanks no longer keeps the card alive.
type HourRow = { i: number; day: string; time: string };
const rows: HourRow[] = (Array.isArray(loc.rules) ? loc.rules : [])
  .map((r: any, i: number) => ({
    i,
    day: String(r?.label ?? '').trim(),
    time: String(r?.value ?? '').trim(),
  }))
  .filter((r: HourRow) => Boolean(r.day || r.time));

const num = (v: any, max: number): number | null => {
  if (v === null || v === undefined || String(v).trim() === '') return null;
  const n = Number(v);
  return Number.isFinite(n) && Math.abs(n) <= max ? n : null;
};
const lat = num(loc.lat, 90) ?? num(lay.contact?.lat, 90);
const lng = num(loc.lng, 180) ?? num(lay.contact?.lng, 180);
const hasCoords = lat !== null && lng !== null;
const hasAddress = !!address;
// The drawn face is a picture claiming where this café physically is. With
// neither coordinates nor an address there is nothing real behind that claim, so
// the whole cell leaves and the card takes the full width.
const hasMap = hasCoords || hasAddress;

// The pin bubble and the marker popup. No placeholder: with no business name the
// bubble leaves and the pin is a bare dot, rather than a label reading "Your
// Business" over a real street.
const brand = String(lay.businessName ?? '').trim();
// The design's "cafeTown". Nothing derives it, so on a site without a city it is
// simply absent rather than guessed out of the address.
const locality = String(lay.contact?.city ?? '').trim();

const hasCard = rows.length > 0 || !!hoursNote;
const hasSplit = hasMap || hasCard;
const hasLoc = hasSplit || !!sub;
---
{hasLoc && (
<section class="loc" id="location">
  <div class="wrap">
    {/* TWO children, exactly as the design draws it: the heading block and the
        arrival note beside it. No address paragraph — see the frontmatter. */}
    <div class="loc-head reveal">
      <div class="loc-head-l">
        {tag && <span class="kick" data-field="location.tag">{tag}</span>}
        {headline && <h2 class="h2 loc-h2" data-field="location.headline" set:html={headline}></h2>}
      </div>
      {sub && <p class="body loc-sub" data-field="location.sub">{sub}</p>}
    </div>
    {hasSplit && (
      <div class:list={['loc-split', 'reveal', (!hasMap || !hasCard) && 'one-up']} data-delay="1">
        {hasMap && (
          <div class="map-frame">
            <div id="map">
              {/* Tier-3 only, and entirely decorative: PageBL's boot empties #map
                  before Leaflet draws, so tiles never stack on top of this. One
                  aria-hidden, here and nowhere else in the subtree. */}
              <div class="map-face" aria-hidden="true">
                <span class="road road-h"></span>
                <span class="road road-v"></span>
                <span class="pin">
                  <span class="dot"></span>
                  {brand && <span class="plabel">{brand}</span>}
                </span>
              </div>
            </div>
            {/* OUTSIDE #map on purpose: the city is the owner's real text, not
                fallback chrome, so it has to survive the boot that erases the
                face — and it stays in the accessibility tree. */}
            {locality && <span class="mono map-cap">{locality}</span>}
          </div>
        )}
        {hasCard && (
          <div class="hcard">
            {rows.map((r) => (
              /* Both cells always render, even when one is blank: the row is
                 space-between, so dropping an empty cell would promote its
                 sibling to the wrong edge. An empty span draws nothing — the
                 guard that matters ran above, where a wholly blank row was
                 dropped along with its hairline. */
              <div class="rowrule hrow">
                <span class="hd" data-field={`location.rules.${r.i}.label`}>{r.day}</span>
                <span class="ht" data-field={`location.rules.${r.i}.value`}>{r.time}</span>
              </div>
            ))}
            {hoursNote && <p class="hnote" data-field="location.hours">{hoursNote}</p>}
          </div>
        )}
      </div>
    )}
  </div>
</section>
)}
{hasCoords && (
  <script is:inline define:vars={{ lat, lng, brand }}>
    (function () {
      // Bounded like the geocode branch below: this script is emitted where the
      // section sits, ABOVE the wrapper's boot, so one retry is the normal case —
      // but an unbounded loop would spin a timer forever on any page where the
      // wrapper script never ran at all.
      var attempt = 0;
      (function init() {
        if (window.__foodcraftBLInitMap) { window.__foodcraftBLInitMap(lat, lng, brand); }
        else if (attempt++ < 25) { setTimeout(init, 80); }
      })();
    })();
  </script>
)}
{!hasCoords && hasAddress && (
  <script is:inline define:vars={{ address, brand }}>
    (function geocodeThenInit() {
      var attempt = 0;
      function tryInit(la, ln) {
        if (window.__foodcraftBLInitMap) { window.__foodcraftBLInitMap(la, ln, brand); }
        else if (attempt++ < 25) { setTimeout(function () { tryInit(la, ln); }, 80); }
      }
      try {
        var query = encodeURIComponent(String(address).replace(/\n/g, ', '));
        var url = 'https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/search?format=json&limit=1&q=' + query;
        fetch(url, { headers: { 'Accept-Language': 'en' } })
          .then(function (r) { return r.ok ? r.json() : []; })
          .then(function (results) {
            if (!Array.isArray(results) || results.length === 0) return;
            var la = parseFloat(results[0].lat);
            var ln = parseFloat(results[0].lon);
            if (isNaN(la) || isNaN(ln)) return;
            tryInit(la, ln);
          })
          .catch(function () { /* keep the drawn face: it is the design */ });
      } catch (e) { /* never block render */ }
    })();
  </script>
)}
<style>
  /* ── THE HEAD · flex, bottom-aligned, space-between, wrapping before either
        side can overflow. 34px to the split, straight from the design.
        TWO children only. A third — an address paragraph under the h2 — is what
        dragged the sub down a row in the BK sibling, because in this row a
        taller left cell moves everything aligned to its bottom edge. */
  .loc-head {
    display: flex; align-items: flex-end; justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 30px; flex-wrap: wrap; margin: 0 0 34px;
  }
  /* min-width:0 so a long unbroken address cannot widen the cell past the row
     and push the page sideways (R8). */
  .loc-head-l { min-width: 0; }
  /* PageBL's .h2 primitive already carries the design's face,
     clamp(31px,4vw,54px)/1.08 and --ink; only the margin differs — the design
     pushes the h2 .26em BELOW the eyebrow and nothing after it. The primitive's
     24ch measure is kept deliberately: the design sets no max-width here, but
     the head is a two-cell flex row and an uncapped address line would eat the
     cell the arrival note sits in. 24ch is generous for a street line.
     The primitives are :where()-wrapped in the wrapper, so a scoped class here
     out-specifies them without an !important. */
  .loc-h2 { margin: .26em 0 0; }
  /* Qualified with the parent class on purpose: the wrapper's bare-paragraph
     reset is (0,1,1) and it sets a bottom margin this must not inherit.
     .body supplies the design's --ink-soft and its 1.75 leading, but NOT its
     size: .body is clamp(14.5px,1.3vw,15px), which only reaches 15px above a
     ~1150px viewport, where the design states a flat 15px and prints it at
     every width. The size is therefore restated here — exactly as ServicesBL
     (.s-sub) and WhyBL (.why-p) restate it for the same paragraph treatment. */
  .loc-head .loc-sub {
    margin: 0; min-width: 0; max-width: 34ch;
    font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.75;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* ── THE SPLIT · the design's 1.2fr / .8fr with a 24px gutter. Either cell can
        be absent — no coordinates and no address drops the map, no rows and no
        note drops the card — and the survivor takes the full width instead of
        leaving a hole beside itself. */
  .loc-split {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0,1.2fr) minmax(0,.8fr);
    gap: 24px; align-items: stretch;
  }
  .loc-split.one-up { grid-template-columns: minmax(0,1fr); }

  /* ── THE MAP CELL. Not the wrapper's .plate: that frame is for an upload and
        carries --well plus --r-md, where the design draws this cell on --paper-2
        inside a --line hairline at --r-lg. */
  .map-frame {
    position: relative; min-height: 360px; overflow: hidden;
    border-radius: var(--r-lg); border: 1px solid var(--line);
    background-color: var(--paper-2);
  }
  #map { position: absolute; inset: 0; z-index: 0; }
  /* The plotted face: a 44px rust grid with two roads crossing under the pin.
     Token-derived, so an admin accent pick moves the plot lines with the pin. */
  .map-face {
    position: absolute; inset: 0; background-color: var(--paper-2);
    background-image:
      repeating-linear-gradient(0deg, rgba(var(--accent-rgb),.08) 0 1px, transparent 1px 44px),
      repeating-linear-gradient(90deg, rgba(var(--accent-rgb),.08) 0 1px, transparent 1px 44px);
  }
  .road { position: absolute; background-color: rgba(var(--accent-rgb),.22); }
  .road-h { top: 47%; left: 0; right: 0; height: 5px; }
  .road-v { top: 0; bottom: 0; left: 44%; width: 4px; }
  /* The cap sits on the ABSPOS pin, not on the bubble. The stack is CENTRED on
     the 44% crossing, so it only stays inside the frame when it is capped at
     twice the smaller side — 88% — less 12px of air each side. calc(100% - 24px)
     would still let a long café name run past the left edge and be clipped there
     by the frame, which is the truncation the wrapping bubble exists to avoid.
     width: max-content is required alongside it, or absolute shrink-to-fit
     collapses the stack to (containing block - left). */
  .pin {
    position: absolute; top: 47%; left: 44%; transform: translate(-50%,-100%);
    display: grid; justify-items: center; gap: 9px;
    width: max-content; max-width: calc(88% - 24px); text-align: center; z-index: 1;
  }
  .dot {
    width: 14px; height: 14px; border-radius: 50%;
    background-color: var(--accent); border: 3px solid var(--paper);
  }
  /* Espresso bubble, bone label, full pill — the design's own values, and 13.2:1
     whatever the tiles underneath it look like. nowrap is dropped: see the
     frontmatter. */
  .plabel {
    background-color: var(--band); color: var(--bone);
    /* Weight 400, not the wrapper .cap's 600: the design sets a weight on every
       other tracked-caps label in the template and deliberately sets none here,
       so this pill inherits regular. line-height is stated because the wrapper's
       body leading of 1.7 would grow the design's ~21px pill by 4px, and because
       the bubble can now wrap. */
    font-family: var(--sans); font-weight: 400; font-size: 9px;
    letter-spacing: .2em; text-transform: uppercase; line-height: 1.45;
    padding: 5px 12px; border-radius: var(--r-pill);
    text-align: center; overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* .mono supplies the design's 10px caps, --sans and --muted. Four things are
     local, and only the last is an addition:
       · letter-spacing .22em — the design tracks this label wider than .mono.
       · font-weight 400 — the design's caption sets NO weight and inherits the
         body's regular, where .mono carries the wrapper's tracked-caps 500.
         ServicesBL restates the same 400 on .s-tag for the same reason.
       · line-height 1.3 — the design's caps labels set no leading, so they
         compute to `normal` (~1.3 in Space Grotesk). PageBL's body 1.7 leaks
         in otherwise and inflates the pill below from ~23px to ~27px around
         10px of text. Same leak, same fix as TrustBL's .tally-lab.
